    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d547">

                <pb facs="0669" n="663" sameAs="frus:frus1942v03/pg663"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/26084½: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Henderson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-11-28T15:00:00+03:00">November 28,
                            1942—3 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 10:01 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>503. Reference my 464, November 15, 7 p.m. General Hurley, who has been held up
                    here by non-flying weather, departed by Soviet plans with his aide, Major
                        Henry<anchor n="5" xml:id="d547-f1"/> and Acting military Attaché,
                    Lieutenant <pb facs="0670" n="664" xml:id="pg664"/> Colonel Park,<anchor n="6"
                        xml:id="d547-f2"/> for the Voronezh–Stalingrad front. He expects to be on
                    this front about 4 days and then to spend several days on the Caucasian front.
                    He does not know as yet whether he will return to Moscow or proceed from the
                    Caucasus direct to Tehran.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Henderson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
